{
  "an3.37:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 3.37 ",
  "an3.37:0.2": "4. Messengers of the Gods ",
  "an3.37:0.3": "The Four Great Kings (1st) ",
  "an3.37:1.1": "“On the eighth day of the fortnight, mendicants, the ministers and councillors of the four great kings wander about the world, thinking: ",
  "an3.37:1.2": "‘Hopefully most humans are paying due respect to their parents, ascetics and brahmins, honoring the elders in their families, observing and keeping vigil on the sabbath, and making merit.’ ",
  "an3.37:1.3": "And on the fourteenth day of the fortnight, the sons of the four great kings wander about the world, thinking: ",
  "an3.37:1.4": "‘Hopefully most humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit.’ ",
  "an3.37:1.5": "And on the fifteenth day sabbath, the four great kings themselves wander about the world, thinking: ",
  "an3.37:1.6": "‘Hopefully most humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit.’ ",
  "an3.37:2.1": "If only a few humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit, ",
  "an3.37:2.2": "the four great kings address the gods of the thirty-three, seated together in the Hall of Clear Right: ",
  "an3.37:2.3": "‘Only a few humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit.’ ",
  "an3.37:2.4": "Then the gods of the thirty-three are disappointed, thinking, ",
  "an3.37:2.5": "‘The heavenly hosts will dwindle, while the titan hosts will swell!’ ",
  "an3.37:3.1": "But if many humans are paying